{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01NrQTug81jtj39PjErTbou8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RGE2b3b1pKbGtFhjUWS92U","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"symb_invariant_consensus","tier":"worst","dream_id":"dream_20260516T054054","overall_score":"Weak Reject (3/6)","report":"# Reviewer 2 (R2): Novelty / Originality & Related Work / Positioning\n\n## Paper: \"Why Symbolic Consensus Fails: A Diagnostic Framework for Constraint-Based LLM Aggregation\"\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\nThe paper proposes SICA, a symbolic consensus method that extracts FOL constraints from LLM reasoning traces and uses weighted partial MAX-SAT to aggregate answers, as an alternative to Self-Consistency majority voting. The main empirical finding is negative: SICA yields +0.7pp over SC on 804 questions (p=0.32), statistically insignificant. The paper reframes this as a diagnostic contribution, introducing C0 (multi-candidate rate) as a zero-cost predictor of when any consensus method can help, and characterizing the \"operating regime\" where symbolic consensus is viable. The tiebreak mechanism is directionally correct (60% vs 50%) but applies to only 2.5% of questions.\n\n---\n\n## Strengths\n\n- **Honest reporting of negative results.** The paper does not oversell. Presenting a predominantly negative finding with careful analysis of why the method fails is valuable and underrepresented in the literature. The community benefits from knowing that this natural-sounding hypothesis (symbolic structure should help consensus) does not pan out in practice.\n\n- **The \"operating regime\" framing is conceptually sound.** Characterizing *when* a method works rather than just *whether* it works is a mature scientific stance. The observation that reasoning models eliminate trace diversity entirely (C0 < 5%) is a genuinely informative finding about the nature of these models.\n\n- **C0 as a necessary-condition screen has practical value.** The asymmetric interpretation -- low C0 reliably predicts gap=0, but high C0 does not guarantee gains -- is correctly stated and useful. The zero-cost property (computed from SC votes alone) is attractive.\n\n- **Thorough experimental decomposition.** The K-sensitivity, tiebreak strategy comparison, and model scaling analyses are well-structured. Table 5 (model scaling) clearly demonstrates the \"capability trap\" phenomenon.\n\n- **The capability trap insight is interesting.** The observation that SICA helps most where models are weakest, but constraint quality is also lowest there, identifies a genuine Catch-22 for neuro-symbolic consensus methods.\n\n---\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n### Novelty Concerns (Major)\n\n- **C0 is a trivially derived metric.** The multi-candidate rate is simply the fraction of questions with >1 distinct answer across K samples. This is the most obvious statistic one would compute from any SC run. Calling this a \"diagnostic framework\" significantly overstates the contribution. Any practitioner examining SC vote distributions would naturally look at this. The paper provides no evidence that C0 was not already informally used or discussed in prior work on SC.\n\n- **The \"operating regime\" formalization is underspecified.** Section 2.3 defines three conditions (diversity, tie fraction, constraint quality) with thresholds tau_diversity, tau_tie, tau_quality, but these thresholds are never formally derived -- they are read off from a small number of empirical data points. With only 12 data points in the C0 correlation analysis (Table 7), and Pearson r=0.45 / R^2=0.20, the \"formalization\" is really just an empirical observation dressed in mathematical notation.\n\n- **The core method (SICA) is not novel to this paper.** The paper appears to evaluate an existing method and report that it does not work. If SICA was proposed elsewhere, the paper should cite that work. If SICA is proposed here, then the paper is simultaneously introducing a method and declaring it fails -- an awkward position that undermines confidence in the method design. The paper is ambiguous on this point.\n\n- **Negative result without actionable alternative.** The paper identifies when SICA fails but offers no path forward. A stronger contribution would propose modifications to SICA that address the identified failure modes (e.g., methods to increase effective C0, improved constraint extraction, or hybrid approaches).\n\n### Related Work Concerns (Major)\n\n- **Only 7 references is severely insufficient for a top venue.** The paper cites Wang et al. (2023), Chen et al. (2023), Han et al. (2022), Tafjord et al. (2021), Li et al. (2023), Wei et al. (2022), and Qwen Team (2024). This is far below the standard for EMNLP, where papers typically cite 30-50 references.\n\n- **No Related Work section.** The paper has no dedicated Related Work section at all. Related work is implicitly referenced in the Introduction. This is a significant structural deficiency.\n\n- **Critical missing references in neuro-symbolic LLM reasoning:**\n  - Logic-LM (Pan et al., EMNLP Findings 2023) -- the closest architectural predecessor, which uses LLM-to-FOL-to-solver pipelines\n  - SatLM (Ye et al., NeurIPS 2023) -- SAT solvers for LLM-generated logical formulas\n  - FoVer (TACL 2025) -- FOL verification for natural language reasoning on ProofWriter/FOLIO\n  - DyLoS (Xu et al., EACL 2026) -- adaptive LLM-symbolic reasoning evaluated on the same benchmarks\n  - Aristotle (ACL 2025) -- logic-complete decomposition with strong ProofWriter/FOLIO results\n  - LogicAgent (2025) -- multi-perspective logical reasoning, SOTA on FOLIO\n\n- **Critical missing references in SC variants and weighted voting:**\n  - Taubenfeld et al. (ACL Findings 2025) -- confidence-weighted SC, directly relevant as an alternative weighting scheme\n  - RASC (Wan et al., 2024) -- reasoning-aware self-consistency\n  - Loo et al. (arXiv 2025) -- shows SC gains plateau for strong models, directly supports the QwQ-32B finding\n\n- **Critical missing references in test-time compute scaling:**\n  - Snell et al. (ICLR 2025 Oral) -- foundational test-time compute scaling work\n  - Diversity-Aware Training for Test-Time Scaling (2025) -- directly supports the trace diversity collapse observation\n\n- **Missing references on LLM ensemble methods:**\n  - Mixture-of-Agents (Wang et al., 2024)\n  - LLM-Blender (Jiang et al., ACL 2023)\n\n- **No engagement with reasoning model literature:**\n  - DeepSeek-R1 (Nature 2025) -- defines the reasoning model paradigm\n  - \"Base Models Know How to Reason, Thinking Models Learn When\" (2025) -- explains why reasoning models converge\n\n- **The paper does not position itself against any benchmark SOTA on FOLIO/ProofWriter.** Recent methods achieve substantially higher accuracy on these benchmarks. Without this context, the reader cannot assess whether the 14B-Instruct baselines are competitive or whether SICA is being evaluated in a regime where the benchmarks are effectively solved.\n\n### Positioning Concerns (Moderate)\n\n- **The \"diagnostic framework\" framing oversells.** A diagnostic framework typically provides a systematic methodology, tools, or decision procedures. This paper provides one metric (C0) and a qualitative observation about three conditions. The word \"framework\" implies more structure than is delivered.\n\n- **Inconsistent narrative across the paper.** The title says \"Why Symbolic Consensus Fails,\" but the paper then argues SICA's tiebreak is \"directionally correct.\" The abstract says \"findings are predominantly negative\" but then claims three contributions. This creates tension: is this a negative result paper or a methods paper? The framing tries to be both and ends up being neither convincingly.\n\n- **The \"falsification conditions\" (Contribution 3) are not falsification in the Popperian sense.** The paper identifies empirical conditions where the method does not help, but these are not logical falsification conditions. The method could potentially work under these conditions with better constraint extraction. Calling them \"falsification conditions\" is misleading.\n\n### Statistical and Methodological Concerns (Moderate)\n\n- **Single model family (Qwen).** All four models are from the Qwen family. The operating regime characterization may be Qwen-specific. The literature_map.yaml (which the paper does not use) already identifies Mistral, LLaMA, and DeepSeek-R1 as needed for credibility.\n\n- **Pilot vs expanded discrepancy unexplained.** The pilot (60 questions, C0=53%) shows +3.3pp, while the expanded (804 questions, C0=28%) shows +0.7pp. The paper attributes this to \"selection bias\" but does not explain why the pilot was biased or how the 60 questions were selected. If the pilot was cherry-picked, this undermines the entire narrative arc.\n\n- **C0 correlation is weak.** Pearson r=0.45 and R^2=0.20 across 12 data points is not a strong predictive relationship. Claiming C0 as a \"diagnostic\" based on this is a stretch. The paper acknowledges this (AUC=0.72, 25% false positive rate) but still foregrounds C0 as a main contribution.\n\n---\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n1. Is SICA a new method proposed in this paper, or was it proposed elsewhere? If new, why not iterate on the design to address the identified failure modes before publishing?\n\n2. The pilot 60 questions had C0=53% while the expanded 804 questions had C0=28%. How were the pilot questions selected? Was the selection process aware of C0 or trace diversity?\n\n3. The paper claims C0 is a \"zero-cost diagnostic\" -- but practitioners already examine vote distributions when running SC. What does C0 add beyond what any careful practitioner would already observe?\n\n4. Have you evaluated SICA with non-Qwen models (Llama, Mistral)? The operating regime characterization rests entirely on one model family.\n\n5. The tiebreak win rate is 60% on 20 questions (12/20). A binomial test gives p=0.25. How do you justify claiming the tiebreak mechanism is \"directionally correct\" when the evidence is not statistically significant?\n\n6. Why does the paper have no Related Work section? How does SICA compare to Logic-LM, SatLM, FoVer, and other neuro-symbolic reasoning methods that use similar FOL+solver pipelines on the same benchmarks?\n\n---\n\n## Missing Related Work (Summary)\n\nThe paper's 7 references are critically insufficient. At minimum, the following categories need coverage:\n\n1. **Neuro-symbolic LLM reasoning** (Logic-LM, SatLM, FoVer, DyLoS, Aristotle, LogicAgent) -- 0 of ~6 cited\n2. **SC variants with weighting/confidence** (Taubenfeld et al., RASC, DSC) -- 0 of ~3 cited\n3. **Test-time compute scaling** (Snell et al., diversity-aware TTS) -- 0 of ~2 cited\n4. **LLM ensemble/aggregation** (MoA, LLM-Blender) -- 0 of ~2 cited\n5. **Reasoning model paradigm** (DeepSeek-R1) -- 0 of ~1 cited\n6. **SC scaling analysis** (Loo et al. 2025) -- 0 of ~1 cited\n\nA paper claiming to characterize the operating regime of symbolic consensus methods must engage with the broader landscape of methods that solve or approach the same problem.\n\n---\n\n## Score: 3 (Weak Reject)\n\n## Confidence: 4/5\n\n### Justification\n\nThe paper asks a good question (\"when does symbolic consensus help?\") and provides honest empirical work. However, the contribution falls below the bar for a top venue on multiple dimensions:\n\n1. **Novelty is insufficient.** C0 is a trivial metric; the operating regime \"formalization\" is an empirical observation on 12 data points with weak correlation (r=0.45); the method itself either already exists or is introduced and immediately declared non-functional.\n\n2. **Related work is critically deficient.** 7 references with no Related Work section is unacceptable. The paper ignores the entire body of neuro-symbolic reasoning literature that uses FOL+solver pipelines on the exact same benchmarks, as well as recent SC variants, test-time scaling work, and ensemble methods.\n\n3. **The negative result, while honest, is thin.** Negative result papers succeed at top venues when they (a) falsify a widely-held belief with rigorous methodology, (b) provide deep mechanistic understanding of the failure, or (c) redirect community effort with actionable alternatives. This paper partially achieves (b) but the mechanistic understanding is shallow (C0 explains only 20% of variance) and provides no actionable alternatives.\n\n4. **Single model family and benchmark-scope limitations** weaken the generalizability of claims about \"operating regimes.\"\n\nThe paper would benefit from: (i) a full Related Work section with 25+ references, (ii) evaluation on non-Qwen models, (iii) either a stronger C0 analysis or honest demotion of C0 from \"framework\" to \"observation,\" and (iv) proposed improvements to address the identified failure modes.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":742,"cache_read_input_tokens":58659,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":742,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":47,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01KRNXEoJX8A5S1JP8jYjMQS","session_id":"8f20b57d-9fbb-4d3c-afd1-df001eab1b14","uuid":"d9541eab-dde3-4b69-a4aa-0a3626fa16f5"}
