{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SU17vrBLtwYn7zSE4wgjj9","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)** | Confidence: 4/5\n\n**Core issues identified:**\n\n1. **Data integrity gap.** The best-tier paper reports +3.8pp (p=0.023) on 804 questions, but the only completed experiment is a 60-question pilot with +3.3pp and p=0.226 (not significant). The median projection for the same 804-question run shows p=0.082 and PW gap of only +0.2pp, far below the best-tier's claimed +1.8pp. The C0-gap correlation inflates from r=0.72 (median) to r=0.94 (best) with no justification.\n\n2. **Mechanism is narrower than claimed.** All SICA-vs-SC differences come from exact vote ties (6:6 splits at K=12). On non-tied questions, SICA=SC identically. The contribution reduces to \"better-than-random tiebreaker,\" not \"constraint-based aggregation\" as framed.\n\n3. **Single model family.** All positive results use Qwen2.5 variants. Planned Mistral/LLaMA/DeepSeek experiments (D021) were never executed. The operating regime claim cannot be validated within one architecture family.\n\n4. **Domain-limited.** ProofWriter consistently shows ~0pp gain (41% constraint precision vs FOLIO's 78%). SICA only works where FOL extraction naturally aligns with the task. No non-logic benchmarks were tested.\n\n5. **C0-gap correlation is methodologically weak.** 5 of 8 data points are from the same model at different K values (not independent), and gap values are discretized to 1.67pp multiples on 60 questions.\n\n**What would improve the score:** Completed 804-question results confirming p<0.05, at least one non-Qwen model with positive results, one non-logic benchmark, and a constraint ablation showing MAX-SAT adds value over simple counting."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a6e49a651df45112c (use SendMessage with to: 'a6e49a651df45112c' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 76432\ntool_uses: 23\nduration_ms: 207215</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8f20b57d-9fbb-4d3c-afd1-df001eab1b14","uuid":"7571a0fb-f69c-4a97-9636-1f713a12d18c","timestamp":"2026-05-16T06:28:48.095Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue review. Your focus areas are **methodology** and **experiments**. Review the paper blindly — do not assume anything about result quality.\n\nRate on a 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept. Target venue acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\nTitle: \"Why Symbolic Consensus Fails: A Diagnostic Framework for Constraint-Based LLM Aggregation\"\n\nThe paper studies SICA (Symbolic Invariant Consensus Aggregation), which extracts FOL constraints from LLM reasoning traces and uses weighted partial MAX-SAT to aggregate answers, compared to Self-Consistency (SC) majority voting. The main finding is negative: SICA provides no statistically significant improvement over SC on expanded benchmarks (+0.7pp, p=0.32). The paper reframes this as a diagnostic contribution with three parts: (1) C0 metric (multi-candidate rate) as zero-cost diagnostic, (2) trace diversity root-cause analysis across reasoning vs instruction models, (3) falsification conditions for when symbolic structure helps.\n\n## Key Results\n\n**Table 1 (Operating Regime):** QwQ-32B reasoning model has C0≈0-18.75% across domains (N=48 total), SICA-SC gap=0 everywhere. Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct has C0=40-63%, gap=+6.7pp on FOLIO-30 but 0pp on ProofWriter-30.\n\n**Table 2 (Expanded 804 questions, 14B):** FOLIO-204: SC=73.5%, SICA=75.5%, gap=+2.0pp, C0=0.32, p=0.18. ProofWriter-600: SC=67.0%, SICA=66.5%, gap=-0.5pp, C0=0.25, p=0.71. Combined-804: gap=+0.7pp, C0=0.28, p=0.32. Pilot D012-60: gap=+3.3pp, C0=0.53, p=0.23.\n\n**Table 3 (K ablation, 60 pilot questions):** K=6: gap=+1.7, tie%=3.3%; K=8: gap=0.0, tie%=1.7%; K=12: gap=+3.3, tie%=6.7%; K=16: gap=+1.7, tie%=3.3%; K=20: gap=0.0, tie%=1.7%. Exclusion rate always <3%.\n\n**Table 4 (Tiebreak, N=20 ties out of 804):** SICA 60% acc on ties, random 50%, alphabetical 45%, first-seen 40%.\n\n**Table 5 (Model scaling, 60 questions):** 7B: SC=58.3%, C0=0.65, gap=+1.7; 14B: SC=70.0%, C0=0.53, gap=+3.3; 72B-AWQ: SC=78.3%, C0=0.18, gap=0.0; QwQ-32B: SC=72.7%, C0=0.036, gap=0.0.\n\n**Table 6 (C0 correlation):** 12 datapoints, Pearson r=0.45, Spearman ρ=0.52, AUC=0.72.\n\n**Table 7 (Overhead):** Generation 85%/49.5s, Extraction 12%/7.0s, MaxSAT 3%/1.8s. Total 2.2x SC cost.\n\n**Qualitative (Sec 4.7):** 55% constraints semantically meaningful, 30% trivially correct (answer restatements), 15% malformed. Exclusion rate 2.2%, justified 70% of time.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (what the full plan imagined)\n\nThe experiment plan includes 9 experiments:\n- exp-001: Expanded 14B on 804 questions (done in paper)\n- exp-002: K ablation (done in paper)\n- exp-003: Tiebreak baseline analysis (done in paper)\n- exp-004: 7B scaling (done in paper, Table 5)\n- exp-005: QwQ-32B operating regime (done in paper, Table 1)\n- exp-006: C0 diagnostic validation (done in paper, Table 6)\n- exp-007: 72B-AWQ (done in paper, Table 5)\n- exp-008: Constraint quality case studies (done in paper, Sec 4.7)\n- exp-009: Overhead analysis (done in paper, Table 7)\n\nAll 9 experiments appear covered.\n\n## Your Review (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nPlease evaluate:\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the SICA pipeline well-defined? Is C0 a meaningful contribution? Is the operating regime formalization rigorous?\n2. **Experimental rigor**: Sample sizes, statistical testing, ablation coverage, baseline comparisons. Are conclusions supported by evidence?\n3. **Reproducibility**: Are enough details provided?\n4. **Weaknesses in methodology/experiments**: What's missing or flawed?\n\nStructure your review as:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted)\n- Questions for Authors\n- Missing experiments or analyses\n- Score and confidence\n\nBe critical but fair. This is a top venue with ~25% acceptance rate.","agentId":"a6e49a651df45112c","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)** | Confidence: 4/5\n\n**Core issues identified:**\n\n1. **Data integrity gap.** The best-tier paper reports +3.8pp (p=0.023) on 804 questions, but the only completed experiment is a 60-question pilot with +3.3pp and p=0.226 (not significant). The median projection for the same 804-question run shows p=0.082 and PW gap of only +0.2pp, far below the best-tier's claimed +1.8pp. The C0-gap correlation inflates from r=0.72 (median) to r=0.94 (best) with no justification.\n\n2. **Mechanism is narrower than claimed.** All SICA-vs-SC differences come from exact vote ties (6:6 splits at K=12). On non-tied questions, SICA=SC identically. The contribution reduces to \"better-than-random tiebreaker,\" not \"constraint-based aggregation\" as framed.\n\n3. **Single model family.** All positive results use Qwen2.5 variants. Planned Mistral/LLaMA/DeepSeek experiments (D021) were never executed. The operating regime claim cannot be validated within one architecture family.\n\n4. **Domain-limited.** ProofWriter consistently shows ~0pp gain (41% constraint precision vs FOLIO's 78%). SICA only works where FOL extraction naturally aligns with the task. No non-logic benchmarks were tested.\n\n5. **C0-gap correlation is methodologically weak.** 5 of 8 data points are from the same model at different K values (not independent), and gap values are discretized to 1.67pp multiples on 60 questions.\n\n**What would improve the score:** Completed 804-question results confirming p<0.05, at least one non-Qwen model with positive results, one non-logic benchmark, and a constraint ablation showing MAX-SAT adds value over simple counting."}],"totalDurationMs":207215,"totalTokens":76432,"totalToolUseCount":23,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3575,"cache_read_input_tokens":72364,"output_tokens":492,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3575},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":492,"cache_read_input_tokens":72364,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3575,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3575,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":9,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":11}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01CyzFZaRAtcnDtNHUQMrDa1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both reviewers have completed their independent reviews. Now I need to conduct the full roundtable process:\n- Step 1: Independent reviews (done)\n- Step 2: Cross-sharing (share each reviewer's findings with the other)\n- Step 3: Convergence (discuss and reconcile)\n- Step 4: Synthesis (final consolidated report)\n\nLet me synthesize the roundtable discussion and produce the final review report, then submit it via the submit_dream_review_report tool.\n\nLet me first compile the full reviews and then do the roundtable steps. \n\nSince both reviewers gave 3/6 (Weak Reject), I need to work through the cross-sharing and convergence steps. I'm compiling the key points from each reviewer to share: R1 identified that all SICA gains come from exact vote ties only, the C0-gap correlation relies on non-independent datapoints with heavy overlap from the same model at different K values, the evaluation uses only a single model family (Qwen), and there's no ablation showing MAX-SAT adds value beyond simple counting. Now I'll synthesize these findings into the final consolidated report.\n\nR2's main concerns center on C0 being trivially derived—any practitioner would compute it—the sparse reference list with massive gaps in related work coverage, the lack of a dedicated Related Work section, the \"diagnostic framework\" label overselling what's essentially a metric plus observations, and an inconsistent narrative where the title claims the approach \"fails\" but the paper argues for its correctness. Both reviewers converge on the 3/6 score, the critical limitation of evaluating only a single model family, the underwhelming nature of C0 as a contribution, insufficient statistical evidence, and the need for substantially more related work. Where they diverge is in emphasis: R1 pushes harder on experimental gaps like missing constraint ablations and non-logic benchmarks, while R2 emphasizes positioning and novelty issues around related work and framing. The consolidated rejection rests on these fundamental limitations across experimental rigor, contribution scope, and scholarly positioning.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":549,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3248,"cache_read_input_tokens":51022,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3248},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8f20b57d-9fbb-4d3c-afd1-df001eab1b14","uuid":"791fac8e-5660-426b-bf86-5e48fc467da0"}
